title: GRC for Cloud
page: grc.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: GRC turns abstract security intent into documented controls, evidence and audit-ready posture; in cloud, controls must be monitored continuously, and because most leave a log, evidence collection is automatable.

== The 30-second version
layout: text
section: top
GRC turns security intent into documented controls, evidence and audit-ready posture.
- Governance: who decides, and who is accountable
- Risk: what could go wrong, how likely, how bad
- Compliance: proving to an auditor that claimed controls run
notes:
GRC stands for governance, risk and compliance. The page calls it the discipline that turns abstract security intent into documented controls, evidence and audit-ready posture.

Governance is who decides, what the policy is and who is accountable. Risk is what could go wrong, how likely it is, how bad it would be, and what we are doing about it. Compliance is whether you can prove, to an auditor or a regulator, that the controls you claim are running.

Cloud changes GRC in two practical ways. Every control is an API call away from drifting, so it has to be continuously monitored, not sampled once a year. And most controls leave a log behind, so evidence collection can be automated.

== The modern cloud GRC stack
layout: layers
section: top
layer: People | Small GRC team | Mapping and exceptions | Auditor relationships
layer: System of record | GRC platform
layer: Live sensing | CSPM or CNAPP
layer: Rules | Compliance-as-code
notes:
The page sums up the modern stack in one sentence, drawn here as layers. Compliance-as-code holds the rules. CSPM or CNAPP does the live sensing. A GRC platform is the system of record. And a small GRC team owns the mapping, the exceptions and the auditor relationships.

The rest of the deck follows the page through each part: what GRC is, the frameworks and how to map them, policy as code, continuous compliance, evidence, platforms, and the people who do the work.

== GRC done well, and done badly
layout: compare
section: what-is
col: Done well | Intent becomes controls, evidence and attestation | Connective tissue between security, the business and auditors | Assurance customers want before they buy
col: Done badly | A separate team with a separate vocabulary | Screenshots for screenshots' sake | A calendar driven by audit windows
The cloud-native version of GRC can avoid most of the failure modes if the team builds with the right primitives from the start.
notes:
GRC translates security intent, such as "we will protect customer data", into controls: specific things we do, like encrypting at rest, MFA on admin accounts and rotating keys. Then into evidence, which is proof those controls run, and into attestation, a third party signing off that what we say is true.

Done well, the page calls it the connective tissue between the security org, the rest of the business, and the outside world of auditors, regulators and customers who want assurance before they buy.

Done badly, it is the worst caricature security has: a separate team, a separate vocabulary, screenshots for screenshots' sake, and a calendar driven by audit windows rather than by what is changing in the environment.

The page's good news is that the cloud-native version of GRC can avoid most of those failure modes if the team builds with the right primitives from the start.

== The three pillars
layout: compare
section: three-pillars
col: Governance | Policies, standards, decision rights, accountability | Who can approve a production deployment? | Outputs: policy, roles, exception workflow, control owners
col: Risk | Threats, likelihood, impact, treatment | Accept, avoid, mitigate or transfer | Outputs: risk register, residual scores, treatment plans
col: Compliance | Controls run as policy requires | Mapped to external frameworks | Outputs: mappings, evidence, gap analyses, audit reports
Most orgs do one well, one passably and one badly. Recognizing which is yours is the first step in maturing the program.
notes:
The G, the R and the C are distinct disciplines that interlock.

Governance is policies, standards, decision-making rights and accountability. It answers the org-chart questions of security: who can approve a production deployment, who owns the IAM model, which exceptions which manager can sign. Its outputs are an information-security policy, role definitions, an exception-approval workflow, and a documented owner for every control.

Risk is identifying threats, assessing likelihood and impact, deciding what to do, whether that is accept, avoid, mitigate or transfer, and monitoring whether the treatments are working. Its outputs are a risk register, residual-risk scoring, treatment plans linked to controls, and a periodic review cadence that survives team turnover.

Compliance is demonstrating that controls run as policy requires, mapped to one or more external frameworks, with evidence an auditor will accept. Its outputs are framework mappings, evidence collected on a defined cadence, gap analyses, and clean audit reports, such as SOC 2, ISO 27001 and PCI DSS, that procurement teams can read.

Most orgs do one well, one passably and one badly. Recognizing which is yours is the first step in maturing the program.

== When one pillar is weak
layout: table
section: three-pillars
row: Weak pillar | What it produces
row: Governance | Inconsistent risk decisions
row: Risk | Compliance work that costs more than it should, with nothing risk-prioritized
row: Compliance | Well-governed, well-risked programs with no report to send customers
The pillars are distinct disciplines, but they reinforce each other.
notes:
The page calls the pillars reinforcing.

A weak governance pillar produces inconsistent risk decisions. A weak risk pillar produces compliance work that costs more than it should, because nothing is risk-prioritized. A weak compliance pillar produces well-governed, well-risked programs that customers cannot buy from, because there is no report to send them.

== What cloud changes about GRC
layout: table
section: cloud-changes
row: Dimension | Traditional GRC | Cloud GRC
row: Control unit | Server, network segment, policy doc | API call, resource configuration, identity
row: Evidence shape | Screenshots, manual exports, attestation letters | API queries, JSON, log streams, config snapshots
row: Audit cadence | Annual or quarterly samples | Continuous: every config change is an event
row: Failure mode | Audit window finds the gap; fix before the next audit | Exploitable in hours; the auditor finding lags the breach
The fundamentals predate cloud by decades. The execution is what changes.
notes:
The fundamentals of GRC predate cloud by decades. The execution looks meaningfully different once your workloads run in someone else's data center, behind APIs that auditors do not have ssh access to.

This is part of the page's comparison table. The control unit moves from a server, a network segment or a policy document to an API call, a resource configuration or an identity. Evidence moves from screenshots, manual log exports and attestation letters to API queries, structured JSON, log streams and signed config snapshots. Audit cadence moves from annual or quarterly point-in-time samples to continuous, because every config change generates an event. And where a gap used to be found in the audit window and fixed before the next audit, a misconfiguration is now exploitable in hours.

Three rows are left off the slide. The change rate goes from quarterly releases and change-advisory boards to hundreds of deploys a day, and controls must keep up. Under shared responsibility, the provider owns part of the stack: your scope shrinks but does not disappear. And auditor knowledge varies: newer firms specialize, while older ones are still learning the cloud model.

== The auditor finding now lags the breach
layout: text
section: cloud-changes
In traditional GRC the audit finding generally arrives before the breach. In cloud, an attacker scanning for public S3 buckets or exposed IMDS can act in minutes. GRC has to keep the cloud's pace, not the auditor's calendar.
notes:
The page says the bottom row of that table matters most. In traditional GRC, the audit finding generally arrives before the breach. In cloud, an attacker scanning for public S3 buckets, exposed IMDS or overly permissive trust policies can act in minutes.

So your control either holds at the moment of misconfiguration, or it does not hold at all. GRC has to keep up with the cloud's change rate, not the auditor's calendar.

== Attestations you will be asked for
layout: table
section: frameworks
row: Framework | What it is | What to know
row: SOC 2 | De facto North American B2B SaaS attestation | Most enterprise procurement teams require Type II
row: ISO 27001 | International ISMS standard | Annex A (2022) lists 93 reference controls
row: PCI DSS | Required if you handle cardholder data | v4.0.1 is the only active version
row: HIPAA / HITECH | U.S. healthcare, PHI safeguards | PHI on a non-BAA service: a quiet path to a finding
row: FedRAMP | U.S. federal cloud authorization | Low, Moderate, High; 12-18+ months typical
notes:
You will eventually be asked about most of these. The page's pragmatic strategy is to pick one as your primary internal framework, map all the others to it, and run a single control set with framework-specific overlays where required.

SOC 2 uses the AICPA's Trust Services Criteria. Security is required; Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity and Privacy are optional add-ons. Type I is a point-in-time design assessment. Type II covers a period, typically 6 or 12 months, and verifies the controls ran.

ISO 27001 is more prescriptive than SOC 2 on management-system structure and less prescriptive on individual technical controls. It is required or preferred outside North America for most enterprise buyers, and increasingly co-required with SOC 2.

For PCI DSS, v4.0 was retired at the end of 2024, and v4.0.1 is a limited revision with no new requirements. Tokenization and scope reduction give the most for the effort.

For HIPAA, all three major clouds offer BAA-eligible services.

FedRAMP's impact levels are drawn from NIST SP 800-53, and Moderate is the most common. Most vendors work with a third-party assessment organization and an agency sponsor. In 2024 the FedRAMP Board replaced the Joint Authorization Board, and FedRAMP 20x is being phased in, so check where it stands before you plan a timeline.

== Common language, baselines and privacy
layout: compare
section: frameworks
col: NIST CSF 2.0 | Not an attestation | The most-cited common-language framework | Six functions, from Govern to Recover
col: CIS Benchmarks | Technical baselines for clouds, Kubernetes and OS images | What most CSPM tools score against by default
col: Privacy laws | GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, LGPD, PIPL and dozens more | Most security controls also serve privacy obligations
Sector rules add more, from SOX and CMMC to NIS2, DORA and country-specific cloud requirements.
notes:
Three more entries from the landscape are not attestations.

NIST CSF is the most-cited common-language framework for security programs. CSF 2.0 organizes work into six functions: Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover. The page calls it useful as the meta-framework that maps SOC 2, ISO 27001 and PCI DSS to a shared vocabulary for executive reporting.

CIS Benchmarks are technical baselines: specific configurations for AWS, Azure, GCP, Kubernetes, Docker, OS images and more. They are the implementation-level baseline most CSPM tools score against by default, and aligning with them produces evidence that crosswalks cleanly to SOC 2, ISO 27001 and PCI DSS.

Privacy laws are distinct from security frameworks but overlap heavily, since most security controls also serve privacy obligations. Data residency, data-subject-rights workflows and lawful-basis tracking are the new asks, and the cloud providers have residency tooling, such as regions and sovereign-cloud offerings, to support them.

Sector rules add more: SOX for public-company financial reporting, NIST SP 800-171 and CMMC for the U.S. defense supply chain, NYDFS Part 500 and others for financial services, NERC CIP for electric utilities, NIS2 and DORA in the EU, and country-specific cloud requirements such as IRAP, ENS, BSI C5 and HKMA.

== The 80/20 of mapping
layout: checklist
section: frameworks
check: Pick one internal source-of-truth control set
check: Map every other framework to it
check: Add framework-specific controls only where the others leave a gap
SOC 2, ISO 27001 and most other frameworks overlap heavily. The page says this decision repays the effort better than any other in a multi-framework program.
notes:
SOC 2, ISO 27001 and most other frameworks overlap heavily. So pick one as the internal source-of-truth control set, and map every other framework to it.

Add framework-specific controls only where the others do not already cover the requirement. The page's examples are PCI DSS network segmentation, HIPAA breach-notification timelines and FedRAMP continuous monitoring deliverables.

The page says this decision repays the effort better than any other in a multi-framework program.

== Control frameworks define the how
layout: table
section: control-frameworks
row: Baseline | What it gives you
row: CIS Benchmarks | Per-platform baselines; the lingua franca of CSPM rules
row: NIST SP 800-53 | U.S. federal catalog; underpins FedRAMP; Rev 5 current
row: CSA Cloud Controls Matrix | Cloud-specific, with crosswalks; used in vendor-risk questionnaires
row: Provider baselines | Practical starting points, well aligned with CIS
row: MITRE ATT&CK Cloud | Not a control framework: the threat model to defend against
Attestation frameworks define outcomes. Control frameworks define how, at the configuration level.
notes:
Where attestation frameworks such as SOC 2 and ISO 27001 define what outcomes you must demonstrate, control frameworks define how, at the configuration level.

The CIS Benchmarks include CIS AWS, Azure and GCP Foundations, CIS Kubernetes, and dozens of OS and application benchmarks. NIST SP 800-53 is the comprehensive U.S. federal control catalog. It underpins FedRAMP and is used widely as a reference even outside government. The CSA Cloud Controls Matrix comes from the Cloud Security Alliance, with crosswalks to most major attestation frameworks, and is used heavily for vendor-risk questionnaires.

The provider-authored baselines are the AWS Well-Architected Framework's Security Pillar, the Azure Cloud Security Baseline and the GCP Architecture Framework's security section. The page calls them practical starting points.

MITRE ATT&CK Cloud is the threat model your controls are defending against. Mapping detections and preventative controls to ATT&CK techniques produces the coverage-gap analyses that auditors and CISOs both find legible.

== Policy can be code
layout: table
section: policy-as-code
row: Engine | Where it applies
row: OPA / Rego | Vendor-neutral: Kubernetes admission, Terraform pre-apply checks, API authorization
row: AWS Config Rules | Resource configurations, continuously and on every change
row: Azure Policy | Audit or enforce at subscription or management-group scope
row: GCP Organization Policy | Constraints at organization, folder or project scope
row: Sentinel | HCP Terraform and Terraform Enterprise
row: CSPM rule libraries | Crosswalks to CIS, NIST, SOC 2, ISO 27001, PCI DSS, HIPAA
One rule checks every bucket on every change, instead of a PDF sampled annually.
notes:
The page calls this the biggest mechanical change cloud brings to GRC: policy can be code. The control "S3 buckets must have public access blocked" can be a one-line rule that runs against every bucket every time anything changes, instead of a sentence in a PDF that gets sampled annually.

Open Policy Agent, with its Rego language, is vendor-neutral, runs anywhere, and is the CNCF graduated standard. It is used heavily for Kubernetes admission control, for Terraform pre-apply checks through Conftest, and for API authorization. Kyverno and Gatekeeper handle Kubernetes admission policy.

Each cloud has its own engine. AWS Config Rules evaluate resource configurations continuously and on every change. Azure Policy audits or enforces at subscription or management-group scope, and initiative definitions bundle controls into framework-aligned sets. GCP Organization Policy enforces constraints, and pairs with Security Health Analytics for detective rules. HashiCorp's Sentinel covers HCP Terraform and Terraform Enterprise.

Every CSPM also ships its own rule library with crosswalks to the common frameworks, and most accept custom rules in the platform's own language.

== Where to enforce
layout: compare
section: policy-as-code
col: Pre-deploy | Gives the most for the effort | Conftest, Checkov, Trivy or OPA on Terraform plans | Admission controllers reject non-conformant resources
col: At deploy | Cloud-native enforcement at the API | SCPs, organization policies, deny-by-default IAM | Holds even if pipeline checks were bypassed
col: Post-deploy | The safety net that catches drift | CSPM, Config Rules, Security Command Center, Defender for Cloud | Auto-remediate high-confidence findings; ticket the rest
The layers are complementary. Skip pre-deploy because the CSPM catches it, and you ship the misconfiguration first.
notes:
The page names three enforcement points.

Pre-deploy is the spot that gives the most for the effort. CI/CD pipelines run Conftest, Checkov, Trivy or OPA against Terraform plans and block the misconfiguration before it ships, and Kubernetes admission controllers reject non-conformant resources before they reach the cluster.

At deploy, cloud-native enforcement at the API, through service control policies, organization policies and deny-by-default IAM permissions, stops the change at the cloud's edge even if pipeline checks were bypassed.

Post-deploy, CSPM, Config Rules, Security Command Center and Defender for Cloud catch the drift. Auto-remediation handles the high-confidence findings, and everything else flows to a ticket queue.

Pre-deploy is cheapest and fastest; post-deploy is the safety net for everything that slips. Skipping pre-deploy because the CSPM catches it means you ship the misconfiguration and rely on the detection being tuned, alerted and acted on, by which point the bucket may already have been scanned.

== CSPM and CNAPP: the live sensor
layout: compare
section: continuous
col: What it gives GRC | Continuous, timestamped evidence by framework | Drift detection as an alert | Risk-prioritized remediation | Exception records and auditor-ready exports
col: What it does not give you | Non-technical controls, such as training and background checks | The policy, the framework mapping, the exception register | The auditor relationship: humans answer auditors
The split: CSPM/CNAPP for technical controls, probably 60-70% of any modern framework, and a GRC platform or wiki for the rest.
notes:
A CSPM inventories your cloud, evaluates every resource against a rule set, and emits findings continuously. When the rules are tagged with the frameworks they satisfy, the CSPM becomes the live sensor for your compliance posture. The page calls it the source of truth for whether a control is passing right now, while the system of record lives elsewhere.

What it gives GRC: every control's pass or fail state, timestamped and queryable by framework. A control that was compliant yesterday and is not today becomes an alert, not a quarterly-audit discovery. A modern CNAPP weighs findings by exploitability and exposure, and GRC inherits that prioritization for free. Exception workflows record the approval, the expiration date, the compensating control and the owner, which are the artifacts auditors want. And most CSPMs produce framework-mapped reports out of the box, so the auditor sees the same evidence the security team sees.

What it does not give you: the non-technical controls, such as background checks, security training completion, vendor risk assessments and business-continuity plans, which no cloud API can see. It does not write the policy, the framework mapping or the exception register; that is GRC-team work. And auditor questions are answered by people, not tools.

The page's pragmatic split is CSPM or CNAPP for the technical controls, probably 60 to 70 percent of any modern framework, and a GRC platform or wiki for the rest, both feeding into the same audit-evidence repository.

== Where cloud audit evidence comes from
layout: table
section: evidence
row: Auditors ask about | Cloud-native sources
row: Identity and access | IAM Access Analyzer, Entra ID and Defender, IAM Recommender
row: Configuration | AWS Config history, Azure Resource Graph, GCP Asset Inventory history
row: Activity | CloudTrail, Activity Log, Cloud Audit Logs
row: Encryption and keys | KMS, Key Vault and Cloud KMS APIs, key-usage audit logs
row: Change management | GitHub/GitLab audit logs, pipeline records, IaC plan history
row: Third-party and vendor | The GRC platform; rarely cloud-native
Activity is the most-requested evidence class in any cloud audit.
notes:
The evidence shape changes in cloud, and so does the work of gathering it. These are the categories an auditor will ask about, and the cloud-native sources that answer them.

Identity and access is who has access to what, with what permissions, since when, and used how recently; last-used and unused-access reports answer the least-privilege question directly. Configuration history re-hydrates what a resource looked like during the audit period without re-running scans. Activity is who did what, when and from where. Encryption evidence comes from the key APIs, plus the key-usage audit logs that prove customer-managed-key claims. Change management ties every production change to a ticket, a reviewer, a CI build and an attested artifact.

Two categories are off the slide. Vulnerability and patching evidence comes from CSPM or CNAPP, Inspector, Defender for Cloud and Security Command Center, tied to your remediation-SLA policy. Incident response evidence is tickets, runbooks executed, post-incident reviews and proof of timely notification, from SIEM incident records and IR ticketing. Third-party evidence, such as the subprocessor list and data-processing addenda, lives in the GRC platform.

== From intent to attestation
layout: figure
section: evidence
figure: grc-chain
Evidence is step 3. GRC platforms wired to cloud APIs now collect most technical evidence; the remaining work is defining the mapping.
notes:
The figure shows the chain from the top of the page in four steps: a security promise, the control that implements it, evidence that the control ran, and a third party signing off. The evidence we just walked through is step 3, and a screenshot with no control behind it fails the chain.

On the evidence step, the page says the GRC platforms that have integrated with cloud APIs collect most of the technical evidence automatically. Vanta, Drata, Secureframe, Hyperproof, ServiceNow GRC, OneTrust and others connect to AWS, Azure, GCP, GitHub, identity providers, MDM, HRIS and ticketing, and refresh evidence on a schedule. The control-engineering work is in defining the mapping; once it is defined, the evidence is mostly free.

== GRC platforms
layout: table
section: platforms
row: Category | Examples | Where it fits
row: Compliance-automation-first | Vanta, Drata, Secureframe, Thoropass, Anecdotes | Fastest path to a first SOC 2 for a SaaS company
row: Integrated GRC | ServiceNow GRC, MetricStream, Archer, OneTrust, LogicGate, Hyperproof | Orgs large enough to have a full GRC function
row: Privacy-led | OneTrust, TrustArc, Securiti | Strong on GDPR, CCPA and data-subject-rights workflows
row: Open-source or DIY | Comply, git-based control libraries, OSCAL | Engineers happy to build the missing automation
The system-of-record layer that lives above the cloud-native tooling.
notes:
The page calls GRC platforms the system-of-record layer that lives above the cloud-native tooling, and sorts them into four categories.

Compliance-automation-first platforms are built around automated evidence collection and SOC 2, ISO 27001 and similar attestations, and the page calls them the fastest path to a first SOC 2 for a SaaS company.

Integrated GRC platforms are heavier enterprise tooling with a broader scope: operational risk, vendor risk, third-party risk and audit management. They are right when your org is large enough to have a full GRC function.

Privacy-led platforms are strong on GDPR, CCPA and data-subject-rights workflows.

Open-source and DIY means Comply, internal git-based control libraries, and controls-as-code in OSCAL, NIST's machine-readable control format. It is right when you have engineers happy to build the missing automation.

== Choosing a platform
layout: checklist
section: platforms
check: Integration depth with your clouds and identity provider
check: Depth on the 2-4 frameworks you will use, not breadth
check: Your auditor's familiarity with the platform
check: Total time-to-attestation
A platform that pulls 80% of evidence automatically is worth 2-3 times one that pulls 30%.
notes:
Integration depth comes first, because automated evidence is the whole value proposition. The page says a platform that pulls 80% of evidence automatically is worth 2-3 times one that pulls 30%.

Many tools support 20 or more frameworks, and you will use 2 to 4, so pay for depth on those rather than breadth across all of them. Auditor familiarity matters too: an auditor who has run a hundred SOC 2s on one platform will give you a cheaper audit than the same auditor's first engagement on another.

On time-to-attestation, the page says the fastest path to a first SOC 2 Type II for a 30-person SaaS is a compliance-automation platform, an experienced auditor and 6 months of evidence collection. It calls anything more complex over-engineered.

== AWS, Azure and GCP side by side
layout: table
section: comparison
row: Capability | AWS | Azure | GCP
row: Policy enforcement | SCPs, RCPs | Azure Policy, Initiatives | Organization Policy
row: Compliance posture | Security Hub CSPM | Defender for Cloud compliance dashboard | Security Command Center
row: Audit evidence | Audit Manager | Purview Compliance Manager | Assured Workloads, SCC reports
row: Identity governance | IAM Access Analyzer | Entra ID Governance, PIM | IAM Recommender
Necessary but not sufficient: a cross-cloud GRC platform fills the gaps, and the human program runs above it all.
notes:
This is an excerpt of the page's one-screen reference to the native GRC-supporting capabilities each cloud ships.

The full table also covers activity audit logs (CloudTrail, Activity Log and Cloud Audit Logs), configuration tracking, regulated-workload boundaries such as GovCloud, Azure Government and Assured Workloads, encryption controls, and data classification with Macie, Purview Information Protection and Sensitive Data Protection. On available attestations, AWS and Azure each list more than 100, and GCP more than 75, including FedRAMP High.

The page's point is that the native tools are necessary but not sufficient. Every cloud will tell you it ships everything a regulated workload needs. In practice, the cross-cloud GRC platform fills the gaps between them, and the human program runs above all of it.

== Who does the work
layout: table
section: roles
row: Role | Their part
row: CISO | Owns the program; signs the security policy
row: GRC lead | Owns mappings, audit calendar, evidence, auditor relationships
row: Security engineer | Builds the IAM, encryption, logging and CSPM controls
row: Risk analyst | Keeps the register, runs assessments, tracks treatments
row: Internal auditor | Runs internal audits before the external auditor arrives
row: Engineering and product | Their cooperation is the bottleneck of any GRC program
At small scale the same work collapses onto fewer people.
notes:
GRC roles look different at different company sizes. At small scale the work collapses onto fewer people rather than disappearing.

The CISO or head of security owns the program at executive level, signs the security policy, owns the risk register at board level, and fronts the auditor relationship for major engagements. The GRC lead owns the framework mappings, the audit calendar and the evidence repository, and it is the role that grows in headcount as the company adds frameworks. Security engineers build the technical implementations of the controls and consult with GRC on framework mapping. Risk analysts maintain the register, run cloud, vendor and application assessments, and track treatments.

Two roles are off the slide. Where GDPR or similar applies, a privacy officer or DPO owns data-subject-rights processes, the record of processing activities and the privacy-impact-assessment cadence. External auditors are contracted, not on staff: a CPA firm for SOC 2, a registrar for ISO 27001, a 3PAO for FedRAMP and a Qualified Security Assessor for PCI DSS.

Engineering and product partners are the people whose work the controls describe. A program adversarial to engineering eventually fails its audits, because evidence stops flowing.

== Four maturity stages
layout: timeline
section: maturity
event: Stage 1: Written | Approved policy, one framework, spreadsheet risk register, manual evidence
event: Stage 2: Automated | GRC platform pulling evidence; first clean SOC 2 Type II
event: Stage 3: Engineered | Compliance-as-code fails the build; risk treatments tied to engineering OKRs
event: Stage 4: Strategic | GRC informs the roadmap; risk in dollars; an advantage in sales
Compliance-as-code without a written policy and a defined control set is automating against nothing. Sequencing matters.
notes:
The page offers a staging model for a cloud GRC program.

Stage 1, written: an approved information-security policy, a partial but real asset inventory, one target framework, usually SOC 2, a risk register in a spreadsheet, and evidence collected manually around audit windows.

Stage 2, automated: a GRC platform pulling evidence from cloud APIs, the identity provider, the code repository and MDM. The first clean SOC 2 Type II has shipped, CSPM runs framework-aligned rule packs, the exception workflow is defined, and the auditor receives evidence through the platform instead of screenshots.

Stage 3, engineered: compliance-as-code in CI/CD, so policy violations fail the build. Controls are mapped 1:1 between the internal control set and 2 to 4 external frameworks, risk treatments are tied to engineering OKRs, and a continuous-monitoring dashboard is visible to executives.

Stage 4, strategic: GRC informs the product roadmap, with data-residency features, regulated-industry offerings and regional expansion. Risk-quantification methods such as FAIR produce dollar-denominated risk inputs to investment decisions, and the program is a competitive advantage in sales.

The skip-stage cost is real. An org trying compliance-as-code without a written policy and a defined control set is automating against nothing.

== Common pitfalls
layout: list
section: pitfalls
item: GRC as a paperwork team separate from engineering
item: Single-framework tunnel vision
item: Manual evidence collection at scale
item: Compliance theater: controls written to pass the audit
item: Letting exceptions accumulate
item: Mapping debt after framework revisions
item: Confusing data residency with sovereignty
The page lists three more: outsourcing the auditor relationship, a CSPM that misses your frameworks, and "AI will write our controls."
notes:
The page lists ten pitfalls.

Treating GRC as a paperwork team separate from engineering fails because controls live in code; adversarial GRC ships bad audits. Pursuing SOC 2 without thinking about the ISO 27001 or PCI DSS that is coming six months later means duplicate work and two control sets, so pick the primary up front and plan the overlays. Screenshots in a shared folder are a Stage 1 pattern, and past 20 controls they become the dominant cost of the program.

Compliance theater is controls written to pass the audit, not to reduce risk: the auditor signs off, and the breach happens anyway. Risk-rank, then implement. Every exception is a risk acceptance with an expiration date; without a workflow that surfaces expiring ones, they accumulate silently until an auditor finds them. Frameworks evolve, mapping drift is silent, and every framework revision needs a planned remap. A region in the right country does not, by itself, address regulator concerns about provider personnel access, foreign legal subpoenas or sovereign-cloud requirements.

The other three: the platform does not replace the human conversation with your auditor, and the same firm year over year is dramatically cheaper than rotating. Every modern CSPM maps to the target frameworks, but verify the depth behind the checkbox: some tools map 90% of CIS but only the highlights of FedRAMP. And AI is a credible assistant for drafting policy language, mapping frameworks and summarizing evidence, but it does not replace the human judgment that decides what risk the org will accept, and the auditor still needs a human signatory.

== How long does a first SOC 2 Type II take?
layout: question
section: faq
Picture a 20-50-person SaaS using a compliance-automation platform. Count from "we should do this" to a signed report.
notes:
This is a question from the page's FAQ. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== 9-12 months, from start to signed report
layout: answer
section: faq
Roughly 3-4 months to readiness, a 6-month observation period for Type II, then 4-8 weeks of audit fieldwork. Skip the platform and add 50%; skip the prior auditor relationship and add another 25%.
notes:
The page's answer, for a 20-50-person SaaS using a compliance-automation platform: roughly 3-4 months to readiness, then a 6-month observation period for Type II, then 4-8 weeks of audit fieldwork. That is 9-12 months in total, from "we should do this" to a signed report.

Skip the platform and add 50%. Skip the prior auditor relationship and add another 25%.

== Should GRC report to the CISO or to Legal?
layout: question
section: faq
Think about where the evidence gets produced, and where the contractual and regulatory decisions get made.
notes:
The second question from the page's FAQ. Again, ask it before showing the answer.

== Either works: follow the dependent decisions
layout: answer
section: faq
Most cloud-native orgs put GRC inside Security, under the CISO. Some larger or heavily regulated orgs keep compliance under Legal or a Chief Compliance Officer. Pick the one that matches where the dependent decisions get made.
notes:
Most cloud-native orgs put GRC inside Security, under the CISO. Some larger or heavily regulated orgs, in financial services or healthcare for example, keep the compliance function under Legal or a Chief Compliance Officer.

The trade-off is that Security-aligned GRC is closer to the engineering work that produces evidence, while Legal-aligned GRC is closer to the contractual and regulatory side. The page says neither is wrong: pick the one that matches where the dependent decisions get made.
